{
  "image_name": "0eb598f4651a4962dbcdce40eae5d5d9.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how apoptosis is triggered through two connected routes, the extrinsic and intrinsic pathways, both converging on executioner caspases and cell death. On the left, an external death ligand binds a death receptor such as FAS, recruiting FADD and caspase 8. This directly activates caspase 3/7 and also cleaves BID to tBID, creating a link to the mitochondrial pathway.\n\nThe center of the image focuses on the intrinsic pathway at the mitochondrion. Internal stress, shown as an apoptotic stimulus like DNA damage, acts through BH3-only proteins and the pro-apoptotic proteins BAX/BAK. Anti-apoptotic BCL-2 family proteins oppose this step. When BAX/BAK are activated, the mitochondrial outer membrane becomes permeabilized (MOMP), allowing release of cytochrome c and SMAC/OMI.\n\nCytochrome c then promotes APAF1-dependent activation of caspase 9, which in turn activates caspase 3/7 to drive apoptosis. SMAC/OMI relieves inhibition by XIAP, which otherwise suppresses caspase activity. Overall, the diagram shows that extracellular death receptor signaling and intracellular mitochondrial stress signaling reinforce one another, with BCL-2 family regulation and mitochondrial permeabilization as key control points.",
